    </p>

    <h2>How does SynthID for video work? Spatio-temporal mathematical embedding</h2>
    <p>
      SynthID for video operates in three distinct dimensions: two spatial dimensions across horizontal and vertical pixel coordinates ($x, y$), and a third temporal dimension across sequential video frames ($t$). Rather than watermarking every frame in isolation, DeepMind's encoder synchronizes perturbations across multi-frame groups of pictures (GOP) to guarantee survival against aggressive temporal video compression.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a video sequence frame $V_t$ at time index $t$, the watermarked frame $V'_t$ is generated according to:
    </p>
    <div class="math-box">
      <code>V'_t(x, y) = V_t(x, y) + &gamma; &middot; S(x, y) &middot; T(t) &middot; M(x, y, t)</code>
    </div>
    <p>
      In this equation, <code>&gamma;</code> represents the global modulation strength (calibrated between 0.010 and 0.018 to avoid visual flicker), <code>S(x, y)</code> is the 2D spatial pseudo-random frequency pattern, <code>T(t)</code> is a low-frequency temporal modulation carrier, and <code>M(x, y, t)</code> is a 3D spatiotemporal masking tensor that concentrates energy in high-motion and high-texture scene regions where human vision is least sensitive.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Worked Example with Real Numbers:</strong> Consider a 1080p video frame block experiencing rapid camera panning with a motion texture masking value <code>M = 0.90</code>, a temporal phase <code>T = +0.82</code>, a spatial key value <code>S = 1.0</code>, a baseline luminance coefficient <code>V = 160.0</code>, and <code>&gamma; = 0.015</code>. The resulting modulated luminance value is:
    </p>
    <div class="math-box">
      <code>V'_t = 160.0 + (0.015 &middot; 1.0 &middot; 0.82 &middot; 0.90 &middot; 160.0) = 160.0 + 1.77 = 161.77</code>
    </div>
    <p>
      The subtle 1.77 unit luminance variation is completely invisible to human viewers at 24, 30, or 60 frames per second. However, when Google's 3D convolutional neural network correlates multiple sequential frames, the cumulative correlation score climbs above the detection threshold of <code>0.80</code>, identifying the video as generated by Veo with near certainty.
    </p>

